---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import appCatalog from '../../../design/app-icons.json';
const appAssets = import.meta.glob('../../../lucent/crates/lucent-design/assets/apps/*.svg', { eager: true, import: 'default', query: '?url' }) as Record<string, string>;
const appAsset = (id: string) => appAssets[`../../../lucent/crates/lucent-design/assets/apps/${id}.svg`];
import nativeApps from '../../../lucent/tests/visual/baselines/apps-last-dark-2x.png';
import nativeWidgets from '../../../lucent/tests/visual/baselines/widgets-light-2x.png';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { tokens, displayValue, cssName, rustName } from '../data/tokens';
const roles = ['primary','on_primary','surface','surface_container','on_surface','error','success','warning','info','focus'];
const spaces = tokens.filter(t => t.name.startsWith('space.'));
const recipe = `use lucent_design::{Theme, component, font, radius};

let theme = Theme::new(settings.light);
let launch = theme.button("Open application", Message::Launch)
    .font(font::BODY)
    .padding_xy(component::launcher::ROW_PADDING_INLINE, component::launcher::ROW_PADDING_BLOCK)
    .radius(radius::BUTTON)
    .background(theme.primary)
    .color(theme.on_primary);

// Shared hover and elevation recipes, including child elements.
theme.apply(launch)`;
---
<Base title="Lucent design system — tokens, components & motion" description="Explore the exact design tokens used by Lucent’s Rust desktop: primitive colors, semantic themes, typography, component geometry and motion.">
  <section class="guide-intro wrap"><a class="text-link" href="/">← Desktop Lab</a><span class="eyebrow">DESIGN SYSTEM / THE VISUAL LANGUAGE</span><h1>Every detail.<br /><span class="mint-text">A shared intention.</span></h1><p>Color, type, space and movement, defined once. These are the tokens behind the Rust desktop—and the starting point for your own components.</p><div class="concepts"><span>{tokens.length} source tokens</span><span>Dark + light semantics</span><span>Rust + CSS outputs</span></div></section>
  <div class="guide-layout wrap">
    <aside class="guide-nav"><span class="eyebrow">IN THE SYSTEM</span><nav aria-label="Design system sections"><a href="#foundations">01 · Token architecture</a><a href="#themes">02 · Color & themes</a><a href="#type-space">03 · Type & space</a><a href="#motion">04 · Motion & density</a><a href="#components">05 · Component recipes</a><a href="#spacing">06 · Rounded spacing</a><a href="#app-icons">07 · App icon catalog</a><a href="#catalog">08 · Token explorer</a></nav></aside>
    <div class="guide-content design-content">
      <section id="foundations"><span class="eyebrow">01 / ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH</span><h2>Primitive → semantic<br />→ component.</h2><p>Primitives describe the available values. Semantic tokens give them a purpose. Component tokens define a specific piece of the interface, including the optical geometry that makes the launcher and widgets feel balanced.</p><div class="token-chain"><div><small>PRIMITIVE</small><code>palette.cyan_300</code><span>{String(tokens.find(t => t.name === "palette.cyan_300")?.value)}</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><small>SEMANTIC · DARK</small><code>theme.dark.primary</code><span>Selected action</span></div><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><div><small>COMPONENT RECIPE</small><code>theme.button(…)</code><span>Primary button</span></div></div><p><code>design/tokens.json</code> is the source. The generator resolves references into typed Rust constants and CSS custom properties. CI checks for stale outputs, broken references and cycles. Desktop components consume tokens; the framework stays independent of this visual identity.</p><CodeBlock code="python3 scripts/generate-design-tokens.py\npython3 scripts/generate-design-tokens.py --check" label="Regenerate and verify" /></section>
      <section id="themes" data-design-theme="dark"><div class="ds-section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">02 / COLOR WITH A PURPOSE</span><h2>Same roles.<br />A different atmosphere.</h2></div><div class="theme-switch" role="group" aria-label="Preview theme"><button type="button" data-theme-choice="dark" aria-pressed="true">Dark</button><button type="button" data-theme-choice="light" aria-pressed="false">Light</button></div></div><p>Components ask for <code>primary</code>, <code>on_primary</code> and <code>surface</code>. Switching themes changes their values together, including icon tints. Text pairs in both themes have automated contrast checks.</p>
        <div class="ds-specimen"><div class="ds-specimen-bar"><span>◉ &nbsp; Lucent</span><span>Design preview</span></div><div class="ds-specimen-content"><span class="eyebrow">A QUIETER WORKSPACE</span><h3>Room to focus.</h3><p>Semantic color keeps every component in the same conversation.</p><div class="ds-app"><span class="ds-app-icon" aria-hidden="true">›_</span><div><strong>Terminal</strong><small>Application · ready to launch</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">↵</span></div><div class="ds-specimen-footer"><span class="ds-status">● All changes saved</span><span class="ds-primary-sample">Primary action ↗</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="semantic-grid">{roles.map(role => <div class="semantic-token"><span class="semantic-chip" style={`background: var(--ds-${role.replaceAll('_','-')})`}></span><code>{role}</code><span data-role-value={role}>{String(tokens.find(t => t.name === `theme.dark.${role}`)?.value)}</span></div>)}</div><p class="small">The specimen previews visual states. Native desktop actions run in the VM. The landing page uses a separate mint-and-ink brand theme from the same token source.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="type-space"><span class="eyebrow">03 / RHYTHM & HIERARCHY</span><h2>Space to breathe.<br />Type that belongs.</h2><p>Dimensions are logical pixels. The compositor’s output scale determines physical pixels. Type roles range from compact captions to the stacked desktop clock.</p><div class="type-specimens">{['caption','body','heading','temperature','timer','clock'].map(role => <div><code>font.{role}</code><span style={`font-size: var(--font-${role})`}>Aa</span><small>{displayValue(tokens.find(t => t.name === `font.${role}`)!)}</small></div>)}</div><h3>The spacing scale</h3><div class="spacing-specimens">{spaces.map(token => <div><code>{token.name}</code><i style={`width: var(${cssName(token.name)})`} aria-hidden="true"></i><span>{displayValue(token)}</span></div>)}</div><h3>Corners, with hierarchy</h3><div class="radius-specimens">{['small','control','panel','widget'].map(role => <div><i style={`border-radius: var(--radius-${role})`}></i><code>radius.{role}</code><span>{displayValue(tokens.find(t => t.name === `radius.${role}`)!)}</span></div>)}</div></section>
      <section id="motion"><span class="eyebrow">04 / MOTION & DENSITY</span><h2>Continuity you can feel.</h2><p>The Rust runtime samples <code>Motion::value(now)</code>. The panel uses a 500 ms spatial curve; selection takes 350 ms; content enters in 320 ms and exits in 190 ms. Retargeting starts at the current value, even halfway through a transition.</p><div class="motion-demo" data-motion-demo><div class="demo-label"><span>SHARED MOTION TOKENS</span><span>Browser illustration</span></div><div class="motion-stage"><div class="motion-orbit" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="demo-shell" id="system-motion-panel"><div class="demo-expanded" inert><div class="demo-mini-tabs"><span>Apps</span><span>Widgets</span></div><div class="demo-app selected"><span class="demo-icon">›_</span><span>Terminal</span></div><div class="demo-app"><span class="demo-icon">◉</span><span>Browser</span></div><div class="demo-search">⌕ <span>Find your next action</span></div></div><div class="demo-dock" aria-hidden="true"><span>⠿</span><span>›_</span><span>◉</span><span>▤</span></div></div></div><div class="demo-controls"><button class="button secondary" type="button" data-motion-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="system-motion-panel">Expand launcher ↗</button><code>motion.panel · 500ms</code></div><p class="demo-note">CSS uses the generated timing tokens. The native client samples the same curve in Rust. Reduced-motion preferences disable this website illustration.</p></div><h3>Sharper at higher density</h3><div class="density-grid"><div><strong>2×</strong><p>Text coverage is rasterized at twice the output’s physical density, then resolved once into a cached texture.</p></div><div><strong>1,024</strong><p>Maximum preview texture edge in pixels. Icons prefer vector or larger originals; mip levels smooth reductions.</p></div></div><p>The framebuffer still matches the output’s integer scale. At 2×, one logical pixel covers four physical pixels. Supersampling text improves coverage; it does not add detail to a low-resolution original. Fractional output scaling and complex-script shaping are not yet implemented.</p></section>
      <section id="components"><span class="eyebrow">05 / BUILD WITH INTENT</span><h2>A recipe, not a bag<br />of magic numbers.</h2><p>The desktop composes framework primitives using <code>lucent-design</code>. Choose a semantic role, then a type, spacing and corner token. Component geometry lives under <code>component.launcher</code>, <code>component.calendar</code> and the other component groups.</p><CodeBlock code={recipe} lang="rust" label="A themed component recipe" /><p>Keep raw values in the token source. A new component-specific dimension should name its purpose—for example <code>component.launcher.row_height</code>. Arithmetic, timekeeping and protocol values remain ordinary code.</p></section>
      <section id="spacing"><span class="eyebrow">06 / CURVES NEED ROOM</span><h2>Padding follows<br />the shape.</h2><p>Capsules need more room at their ends than above and below their contents. The framework exposes <code>padding_xy(horizontal, vertical)</code>; the design system chooses the insets. Backgrounds and hit targets keep their full size.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Horizontal</th><th>Vertical</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Button</td><td>20 px</td><td>8 px</td></tr><tr><td>App, command and widget rows</td><td>16 px</td><td>8 px</td></tr><tr><td>Search field</td><td>16 px</td><td>8 px</td></tr><tr><td>Bar capsule</td><td>12 px</td><td>4 px</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Command icons and labels share a vertical center. The dock derives its width from slots, gaps and equal end insets. Panel contents have a 20 px inset.</p><h3>A shared spatial rhythm</h3><p>Widgets move freely while dragging and snap on release to <code>component.widget_layout.grid_step → space.lg</code>: 16 logical pixels. The saved position uses that grid; screen edges take priority so a widget remains visible. This aligns anchors without forcing every widget to have the same size.</p><p>Terminal window corners use <code>component.window.radius → radius.panel</code>: 20 logical pixels. This matches the maximum supported window-rule radius in the prepared compositor. The generated Hyprland rule applies to terminal classes; the compositor draws and clips those corners. Lucent draws its own panel corners. The integration can remove the rule without replacing user configuration.</p></section>
      <section id="app-icons"><span class="eyebrow">07 / LUCENT APP ICONS</span><h2>One family.<br />Each app distinct.</h2><p>{appCatalog.icons.length} original pictograms share a shape, stroke weight and monochrome treatment. The launcher and dock select the same artwork by desktop-entry ID, so translated app names do not change their icon. These are Lucent interpretations, separate from the applications’ official logos.</p><div class="app-icon-catalog">{appCatalog.icons.map(icon => <figure><img src={appAsset(icon.id)} width="48" height="48" loading="lazy" alt="" /><figcaption><strong>{icon.name}</strong><code>{icon.id}</code></figcaption></figure>)}</div><p>Edit <code>design/app-icons.json</code> to change the artwork or desktop-ID aliases. Glyphs use the theme’s semantic foreground; the tile is a subtle tint of that same color. Geometry and tile opacity live in <code>app_icon.*</code> tokens. Unmapped apps retain their supplied icon; a Lucent application tile is the final fallback.</p><CodeBlock code="python3 scripts/generate-design-tokens.py\npython3 scripts/generate-app-icons.py\npython3 scripts/generate-app-icons.py --check" label="Regenerate tokens, SVGs and the typed Rust catalog" /></section>
      <section id="native-checks"><span class="eyebrow">NATIVE COMPONENT CAPTURES</span><h2>From our renderer.</h2><p>These are fixed Vulkan renders of the Rust components, taken from the visual-test baselines. The app names are fixtures; their icons come from the actual Lucent catalog. Keyboard selection, input alignment, switch geometry and seven visible rows are checked separately from the image comparison.</p><div class="density-grid native-captures"><figure><img src={nativeApps.src} width="1280" height="1160" loading="lazy" alt="Dark native launcher with seven complete rows, keyboard-selected last row and focused search field" /><figcaption>Scrolled launcher · keyboard selection · 2×</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={nativeWidgets.src} width="1280" height="1160" loading="lazy" alt="Light native widget selector with drawn switches and a keyboard-selected Focus widget" /><figcaption>Widget selector · semantic focus · 2×</figcaption></figure></div><p>Shell icons use pinned, Apache-2.0 Material Symbols Rounded SVGs. Images keep their aspect ratio. Focus, caret and toggle geometry use named component tokens. <a href="/docs/#quality">Run the native component checks ↗</a></p></section>
      <section id="catalog" data-token-explorer><span class="eyebrow">08 / THE SOURCE, EXPLORABLE</span><h2>Find your token.</h2><p>This catalog is generated directly from <code>design/tokens.json</code>. Copy a Rust constant path or a CSS custom property.</p><div class="token-filters"><label>Search tokens<input type="search" data-token-search placeholder="Try primary, row_height, motion…" /></label><label>Group<select data-token-group><option value="theme">Semantic themes</option><option value="palette">Primitive colors</option><option value="font">Typography</option><option value="space">Spacing</option><option value="radius">Corners</option><option value="motion">Motion</option><option value="component">Components</option><option value="web">Website brand</option><option value="all">All tokens</option></select></label></div><p class="small" data-token-count aria-live="polite">{tokens.filter(t=>t.name.startsWith('theme.')).length} tokens</p><div class="token-list">{tokens.map(token => <article class="token-row" data-token-name={token.name} hidden={!token.name.startsWith('theme.')}><div class="token-identity">{token.type === 'color' && <i style={`background: var(${cssName(token.name)})`} aria-hidden="true"></i>}<div><code>{token.name}</code><small>{typeof token.raw === 'string' && token.raw.startsWith('{') ? `↳ ${token.raw.slice(1,-1)}` : token.type}</small></div></div><strong>{displayValue(token)}</strong><div class="token-copy-actions"><button type="button" data-token-copy={rustName(token.name)} aria-label={`Copy Rust path for ${token.name}`}>Rust</button><button type="button" data-token-copy={`var(${cssName(token.name)})`} aria-label={`Copy CSS variable for ${token.name}`}>CSS</button></div></article>)}</div><p data-token-empty hidden>No tokens match. Try a different group or search.</p><p class="small" data-token-status aria-live="polite"></p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
  <script src="../scripts/design-system.ts"></script>
</Base>

<style>
.native-captures figure { margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
.native-captures img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: var(--radius-small); }
.native-captures figcaption { margin-top: var(--space-sm); font-size: var(--font-small); }
.app-icon-catalog { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(calc(var(--space-page) * 2), 1fr)); gap: var(--space-lg); }
.app-icon-catalog figure { margin: 0; padding: var(--space-lg); background: var(--web-surface-raised, transparent); border: 1px solid var(--web-border, currentColor); border-radius: var(--radius-control); text-align: center; min-width: 0; }
.app-icon-catalog img { width: var(--app-icon-canvas); height: var(--app-icon-canvas); margin: 0 auto var(--space-sm); }
.app-icon-catalog strong, .app-icon-catalog code { display: block; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.app-icon-catalog strong { font-size: var(--font-small); }
.app-icon-catalog code { font-size: var(--font-micro); margin-top: var(--space-xs); opacity: var(--opacity-muted); }
</style>
